        <p class="demo-caption">Employees who click land on a notice that tells them it was a test and their data is safe. Turning on training opens a four-question course from the same page, without adding a step to the builder.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="col" data-reveal>
        <h3>AI that doesn't feel bolted on</h3>
        <p>
          With the ML team, we built an engine that generates templates from each customer's own threat
          data, starting from a scenario or a real attack Sublime caught.
        </p>
        <p>
          My first design gave AI its own template manager, with filters and metadata. I was wrong
          again, for the same reason. Generation wasn't a new experience; it was another way to pick a
          template. I folded it into the existing picker, with refinements that appear only after an
          attack type is chosen, and a promise of a template in 30 seconds or less.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Stands in for ai-generating.webp: the Generate Template modal's steps on the left, the email preview it fills on the right. -->
      <div class="demo-stage" data-reveal>
        <div class="demo-stage-inner">
          <AiGenerateModal client:visible />
        </div>
        <p class="demo-caption">Generate from a real message in the customer's environment or from a scenario, refine it, and watch the email fill in. Sender details stay editable, but the body only changes by regenerating.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="col" data-reveal>
        <h3>A phishing generator that can't phish</h3>
        <p>
          A tool that writes convincing phishing emails can't become a real phishing tool, so I built
          the guardrails into the interaction model:
        </p>
        <ul class="guardrails">
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">No copying.</span>
            Generated emails reuse an attack's structure, never its words.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">No real people.</span>
            Names and company details only appear as variables.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">Structured inputs.</span>
            Attack type, theme, sender persona, audience, and difficulty give analysts control over generation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">Regenerate and refine.</span>
            Try again with the same or revised inputs, then edit dynamic variables inline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">No free-text editing.</span>
            The email body changes through regeneration, never direct editing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="guardrail-title">Graceful fallback.</span>
            Thin detection history switches off environment-based options.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>A dashboard for leadership</h3>
        <p>
          The GA dashboard answers the question executives actually ask: is our program working? It
          shows campaign reach, click and report rates for a selected period, the latest campaign's
          group results, repeat clickers, and a recommended follow-up.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="demo-stage" data-reveal>
