        <p class="demo-caption">One screen answers leadership's question: is the program working? Totals and rates for any period, who keeps clicking, and a recommended next campaign.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- What I learned -->
      <div class="col">
        <div class="divider"></div>
        <section id="learned" data-reveal>
          <p class="label">What I learned</p>
          <h2>Less control, closer to the flow</h2>
          <p>
            Twice, my instinct was to give analysts more control in a separate space. Both times, the
            better answer was less, inside the flow they already knew. Analysts don't want another tool
            to master. They want the answer.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <!-- Outcome -->
      <div class="col">
        <div class="divider"></div>
        <section id="outcome" data-reveal>
          <p class="label">Outcome</p>
          <p>
            Simulations was a top-three roadmap priority, expected to contribute to ARR, win new deals,
            and keep customers from buying a separate tool. It also made Sublime stickier: the same data
            that stops attacks now tests and trains the people who might fall for them. I left shortly
            before GA, so post-launch numbers aren't mine to share.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

    </main>

